# Who Is Intelligent, He or This Man?

Without astrology, here are enough clues for everyone to decide. Arunagiri of Arcot and Periyannan of Poonamallee studied in the same class. Arunagiri was the wealthy son of motor magnate Gokulanathar; Periyannan was the son of cart-driver Chinnathambi.

Teachers indulged Arunagiri's ignorance because of his father. Asked who ruled Gingee Fort, he replied, "What does it matter to us?" Periyannan, meanwhile, detected errors even in the textbook and solved his classmates' doubts. He scored forty-eight out of fifty and passed the tenth standard with distinction. Arunagiri stopped in the ninth, his failures excused by family privilege. Who, then, was intelligent?

Poverty prevented Periyannan from studying further. Through a recommendation he became a clerk under a tahsildar, writing accounts and letters with honesty and skill from morning until late at night. Arunagiri inherited his father's businesses and wealth. People called him foolish for lending freely and predicted that the fortune would soon dissolve.

Periyannan postponed marriage responsibly until he could support a family. Friends praised his wisdom. Arunagiri celebrated a lavish wedding, though his bride suffered from an illness others had feared. Again people called Arunagiri witless and Periyannan discerning.

But office intrigue closed around Periyannan. A compromising letter written by his hand was discovered in a building-contract inquiry. Though he swore that he had taken no bribe, his superiors branded him a fool, dismissed him, prosecuted him, and sent him to prison. At that same moment America invited Arunagiri to advise on making motor vehicles run better in India. One flew abroad while the other wept behind bars. Who was intelligent now?

After many months Periyannan emerged from prison wasted to skin and bone. Former friends refused him work for fear of scandal. One gave him a little money and told him to open a shop, but first ordered him to buy a splendid garland. The garland was for Arunagiri, returning in triumph.

Arunagiri had devised an innovation in wheels, introduced it into motor manufacture, and accumulated wealth at electric speed. He funded prizes, halls, medals, and a teacher-training school bearing his name. The government invited him to advise how industrialisation might drive out poverty. Years passed, and the once brilliant Periyannan wandered about selling tooth powder.

At a great public celebration, old teachers now claimed they had recognised Arunagiri's brilliance even at school. Poets praised his foreign travel and English wife, while hoping for funds to publish their own books. Periyannan stood at a distance, hurt but silent. A man pestered him with questions about America, geography, his thin body, and his livelihood. Periyannan knew every answer and had read the story of Columbus, yet he said nothing and walked away.

You have heard it all. No horoscope is needed. But by what measure shall we decide: who is intelligent, he or this man?

*(Kanchi, 30 August 1964)*
